{"name":"Buttery","hex":"#ffc283","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Buttery","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A surname from Anglo-Norman."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Buttery"}],"entries":[{"title":"Buttery (bread)","blocks":[{"text":"A rowie, also known as a buttery or Aberdeen roll, is a savoury bread roll originating from Aberdeen, Scotland."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Aberdeen_Buttery.jpg/330px-Aberdeen_Buttery.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buttery_(bread)"},{"title":"Buttery (room)","blocks":[{"text":"A buttery was originally a large cellar room under a monastery, in which food and drink were stored for the provisioning of strangers and passing guests. Nathan Bailey's An Universal Etymological English Dictionary gives \"CELLARIST – one who keeps a Cella, or Buttery; the Butler in a religious House or Monastery.\" As the definition in John Stevens's The History of the Antient Abbeys shows, its initial function was to feed and water the guests rather than monks: \"The Buttery; the Lodging for Guests\". In a monastery a buttery was thus the place from which travellers would seek 'doles' of bread and weak ale, given at the exterior buttery door. The task of doling out this free food and drink would be the role of the butterer. At larger monasteries there would also be a basic hostelry, where travellers could sleep for free."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Schloss_Rochlitz_-_Kellergew%C3%B6lbe_%2801-2%29.jpg/330px-Schloss_Rochlitz_-_Kellergew%C3%B6lbe_%2801-2%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buttery_(room)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Making_Aberdeen_butteries.jpg/500px-Making_Aberdeen_butteries.jpg?utm_source=en.wiki